  <p>
    Ulcerative colitis is where the melanocortin system’s third great role — calming
    inflammation — meets a tissue that can be reached without a systemic drug. The disease
    inflames and ulcerates the lining of the colon and rectum; the melanocortin bet is that
    switching on <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a> in that lining can help the mucosa
    <em>resolve</em> rather than only suppress the immune system that is damaging it.<Cite n={6} />
  </p>

  <h2>What breaks</h2>
  <p>
    Ulcerative colitis is a chronic inflammatory bowel disease confined to the colon’s
    mucosa. Relapsing flares of bloody diarrhoea, urgency and pain track with neutrophil
    infiltration, cytokine release and breakdown of the epithelial barrier. Standard drugs
    range from aminosalicylates and steroids to advanced biologics and small-molecule
    immunosuppressants — powerful tools, but ones that often act systemically. A local
    pro-resolving approach is attractive precisely because the pathology sits in a reachable
    surface.
  </p>

  <h2>The melanocortin mechanism</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a> is a potent anti-inflammatory peptide, and much of that
    work on immune cells runs through MC1R: less IL-1β, IL-6, TNF-α and CCL2, and more
    efferocytosis — the clearing of dying cells that lets inflamed tissue finish the job
    rather than linger in a suppressed half-state.<Cite n={2} /><Cite n={6} /> That
    distinction between resolution and suppression is the central idea of the{' '}
    <a href="/inflammation">inflammation</a> page, and it is why a selective MC1R agonist
    can be aimed at the gut without touching appetite (<a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a>) or
    the adrenal axis (<a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a>).
  </p>

  <h2>What the pipeline is doing</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/pl-8177">PL-8177</a> is Palatin’s investigational oral cyclic heptapeptide —
    a potent, selective MC1R agonist enclosed in a polymer coat that releases it in the
    colon. In rats and dogs the drug concentrates in the colon; in a human microdose study
    radiolabelled PL-8177 appeared in faeces and not in plasma or urine, which means it
    acted and broke down inside the gut.<Cite n={1} /> In rat colitis models it preserved
    colon structure and barrier and moved tissue gene expression back toward healthy
    controls — the molecular signature of resolution.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The human signal is small but striking. A randomised, placebo-controlled Phase 2 study
    in active ulcerative colitis reported clinical remission in 33% of treated patients
    (three of nine) versus none on placebo after eight weeks, clinical response in 78%
    versus 33%, and no drug-attributed adverse events.<Cite n={3} /> The honest caveat:
    Palatin stopped enrolment at 12 patients rather than the planned 28 while redirecting
    resources toward obesity programmes and out-licensing, so the study was not powered to
    prove its result.<Cite n={4} /><Cite n={5} /> As of 2026 the programme is available for
    a partner.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Ulcerative colitis is a clean test of whether melanocortin resolution pharmacology can
    work as a local gut drug. The biology and animal data are coherent; the human evidence
    is early and underpowered. For the molecule see <a href="/pl-8177">PL-8177</a>; for the
    ocular cousin of the same idea see <a href="/dry-eye-disease">dry eye disease</a> and{' '}
    <a href="/pl-9643">PL-9643</a>; for the wider resolution cluster see{' '}
    <a href="/resomelagon">resomelagon</a> and the <a href="/pipeline">pipeline</a>.
  </p>

